WEBVTT

00:00:03.096 --> 00:00:08.266
Every session has three phases: opening, holding and closing.

00:00:08.316 --> 00:00:12.046
Each has a few simple moves that make a big difference.

00:00:12.096 --> 00:00:17.026
Opening. Start with a check-in: a quick round where everyone says something.

00:00:17.076 --> 00:00:20.786
For example: “In one word, how do you arrive today?”

00:00:20.836 --> 00:00:27.826
It sounds small, but it gets every voice into the room early, and people who have spoken once speak more easily later.

00:00:27.876 --> 00:00:34.086
Then say why you’re here, what you’ll do, and what you hope to have at the end. Show the agenda.

00:00:34.136 --> 00:00:41.326
And agree on one or two ground rules, like “phones away” or “we listen to understand, not to reply.”

00:00:41.376 --> 00:00:45.006
Holding. This is the middle part, where the work happens.

00:00:45.056 --> 00:00:46.966
Three moves help you most.

00:00:47.016 --> 00:00:49.546
One: write before talking.

00:00:49.596 --> 00:00:53.686
Give people two or three minutes to write their own thoughts on post-its first.

00:00:53.736 --> 00:00:57.626
You’ll get more ideas, and quieter people get a fair chance.

00:00:57.676 --> 00:01:06.106
Two: use rounds. Go around the room so everyone shares one thing, instead of an open discussion where the same three people talk.

00:01:06.156 --> 00:01:08.946
Three: keep time, out loud.

00:01:08.996 --> 00:01:11.246
“We have ten minutes left for this part.”

00:01:11.296 --> 00:01:16.126
A visible time box keeps energy up and stops discussions from going in circles.

00:01:16.176 --> 00:01:20.746
If something important comes up that doesn’t belong here, put it on a “later” sheet.

00:01:20.796 --> 00:01:23.846
It shows you heard it, without derailing the session.

00:01:23.896 --> 00:01:27.106
Closing. Never let a session just run out.

00:01:27.156 --> 00:01:29.006
Leave ten minutes at the end.

00:01:29.056 --> 00:01:30.806
Read back what you agreed.

00:01:30.856 --> 00:01:34.066
Ask: “Is there anything on here you can’t live with?”

00:01:34.116 --> 00:01:37.346
Agree who writes it up, and when you’ll look at it again.

00:01:37.396 --> 00:01:40.906
And end with a check-out round: “What do you take with you?”

00:01:40.956 --> 00:01:45.406
It tells you how the session landed, and it ends things on a human note.

00:01:45.456 --> 00:01:47.826
Opening, holding, closing.

00:01:47.876 --> 00:01:52.363
If you only remember one thing from this lesson: protect the ten minutes at the end.

